(a) The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building, or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use if he finds that conditions exist in such building, dwelling, or structure which are dangerous or injurious to the health or safety of the occupants of such dwelling, building, or structure; of the occupants of neighborhood dwelling, buildings, or structures; or of other residents of the city. Such conditions include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing):
(1) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents or other calamities;
(2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light, or sanitary facilities;
(3) Dilapidation;
(4) Disrepair;
(5) Structural defects;
(6) Uncleanliness; and
(7) Other additional standards which may from time to time be adopted and referenced herein by ordinance or amendment.
(b) The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building, or structure is vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes based upon personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed.
(Ord. No. 06-14-CCO, § 13-4, 12-2-2014)

626:
In addition to any other powers granted elsewhere under this chapter, the public officers designated in this chapter shall have the following powers:
(1) To investigate the dwelling conditions in the city in order to determine which dwellings, buildings, or structures therein are unfit for human habitation or are unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use or are vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes;
(2) To administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;
(3) To enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations; provided, however, that such entries shall be made in such manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession;
(4) To appoint and fix the duties of such officers, agents, and employees as he deems necessary to carry out the purposes of the chapter;
(5) To delegate any of his functions and powers under the chapter to such officers and agents as he may designate; and
(6) To take any other lawful actions which are necessary or convenient to carry out and effectuate the provisions of O.C.G.A. §§ 41-2-7—41-2-17 or this chapter.
(Ord. No. 06-14-CCO, § 13-5, 12-2-2014)

628:
A nuisance is anything which tends to lead to the immediate annoyance of the citizens in general, or which is manifestly injurious to the public health and safety, or tends greatly to corrupt the morals and manners of the people, with exception given to those rights protected in the Constitution. Nuisances include, but are not limited to:
(1) Stagnant water on premises;
(2) Dead or decaying matter left unattended;
(3) Weeds or grass in excess of eight inches on lots with a residence. For vacant lots in platted subdivisions, the limit for grass and weeds is 12 inches on the property. This requirement does not apply to properties used for agricultural purposes. The public officer shall have the discretion to waive this requirement to allow vacant lots to return to their natural state when:
a. The configuration of the lots and the history of development within the subdivision are such that no substantial harm is caused to other lots in the subdivision and that development of the vacant lots is not imminent; or
b. Additional green space will be created within a subdivision. The purpose of this waiver is to recognize that in some limited circumstances, it may be more appropriate to allow undeveloped lots to return to their natural state than to require unwanted and unnecessary maintenance.
(4) Excess smoke or fumes caused by burning;
(5) Maintaining a dangerous or diseased animal;
(6) Maintaining chicken coops or other animals which tend to cause excessive odor or sound;
(7) Obstruction of a public street, highway, sidewalk, or public right-of-way without a permit;
(8) Walls, trees, or buildings that present an impending danger to the public;
(9) Any trees, shrubbery, or other plants which tend to obstruct clear, safe vision of roadways and intersections within the city;
(10) Non-functioning appliances, major auto repairs, non-licensed and/or inoperable vehicles, or similar unsightly objects which are visible from the public street or neighboring property. Each of these objects must be stored in an enclosed accessory building or in an area not visible from public streets or neighboring properties;
(11) Yard sales lasting longer than eight hours in any 24-hour period;
(12) Debris or other household items that can be seen from the public street or neighboring property;
(13) The pollution of public water or the injection of matter into the sewer system which would be damaging thereto;
(14) Any major auto repairs such as building motors, transmissions, or heavy body work visible from the public street or a neighboring property. Such work shall be done in an enclosed accessory building or on a section of property not visible from the public street or neighboring property;
(15) Abandoned mobile homes, manufactured homes, or recreational vehicles. No abandoned mobile, manufactured homes or recreational vehicle small be used as storage or accessory building;
(16) Any other condition constituting a nuisance under state law.
(Ord. No. 06-14-CCO, § 13-7, 12-2-2014; Ord. No. 0-5-2021, §§ 1, 2, 4-13-2021)
